    "contentEn": "<p>A private transfer from Valencia Airport (VLC) to Only YOU Hotel Valencia covers about 12 kilometres and takes around 25 minutes by car, running from the terminal into the heart of Ciutat Vella, Valencia's old town, where the hotel sits barely 100 metres from Plaza del Ayuntamiento. Your driver waits at arrivals with a name board, tracks your flight in real time so pickup adjusts automatically if you land early or late, and takes you directly toward the hotel entrance rather than leaving you to find your own way with luggage.</p>\n<p>Because the hotel sits deep inside the pedestrian-oriented streets of the old town, the final stretch of the journey brings the car through the wider avenues bordering Ciutat Vella before a short last approach toward Plaza del Ayuntamiento, Valencia's grand central square. A driver who knows this part of the city well times that last stage to get as close to the entrance as the street layout allows, which matters more here than on routes ending on an open modern avenue. You can check live arrival information on <a href=\"https://www.aena.es\">aena.es</a> before you fly, and arrange the whole journey — pickup point, flight number, and price — in advance through <a href=\"/get-a-quote/\">get a quote</a>.</p>\n<p>The same private service runs in reverse for your departure, with a driver collecting you from the hotel at the time you specify and taking you straight back to the terminal. For travellers arriving after a long flight, swapping a shared shuttle or a taxi queue for a direct, pre-arranged car makes a genuine difference to how the first hour in the city feels.</p>\n<h2>About Only YOU Hotel Valencia</h2>\n<p>Only YOU Hotel Valencia occupies one of the most central addresses in the city, sitting around 100 metres from Plaza del Ayuntamiento — Valencia's principal square, framed by the grand town hall building — and about 500 metres from the Cathedral and La Lonja de la Seda, the city's UNESCO-listed silk exchange. Few hotels in Valencia are within easy walking distance of all three landmarks at once.</p>\n<p>The building itself carries a specific piece of hotel history: it stands on the site of what was Valencia's original first luxury hotel, a property that once set the standard for high-end accommodation in the city, long before the current wave of design-led openings. Rebuilding a contemporary lifestyle hotel on that same ground gives Only YOU Hotel Valencia a layered identity — a genuinely historic hospitality address reinterpreted for a modern stay.</p>\n<p>The hotel belongs to the Only YOU Hotels collection, the lifestyle-focused brand developed by Palladium Hotel Group, one of Spain's larger hospitality companies. The brand is built around contemporary interiors, a strong connection to the specific neighbourhood each property sits in, and a positioning aimed at travellers who want their hotel to feel like part of the city's current identity rather than a conventional, formal city hotel.</p>\n<p>That combination — a lifestyle-brand interior on a site tied to the city's hotel history, right in the middle of Ciutat Vella — is really the core appeal of the property. Guests get contemporary design credentials without sacrificing proximity to the oldest and most historically dense part of Valencia.</p>\n<h3>Who This Transfer Suits</h3>\n<p>Design-conscious travellers and couples looking for a city break centred on Valencia's old town are the clearest fit for Only YOU Hotel Valencia, given how directly both the location and the brand's contemporary style play to that kind of trip. Visitors who want to walk to the Cathedral, La Lonja de la Seda, and Plaza del Ayuntamiento without relying on taxis for daytime sightseeing also benefit enormously from the address.</p>\n<p>Travellers arriving with a large amount of luggage should note that the final approach runs through some of the old town's narrower, more pedestrian-oriented streets, so the drop-off point may be a short walk from the door depending on the access allowed at the time. Your driver will always get as close as the street layout permits and help with bags for that final stretch.</p>\n<h3>Landmarks Within Walking Distance</h3>\n<p>La Lonja de la Seda, Valencia's late-Gothic silk exchange completed at the end of the 15th century, is one of the finest examples of civil Gothic architecture in Europe and sits roughly 500 metres from the hotel, an easy walk through the old town. The Cathedral, built over the site of a former mosque and mixing Romanesque, Gothic, and Baroque elements across different centuries of construction, stands just as close.</p>\n<p>Plaza del Ayuntamiento itself, 100 metres from the hotel door, is Valencia's civic heart and the stage for major city events throughout the year, including the mascletà firework displays during the Fallas festival each March. Having it effectively as a front garden means the square becomes the natural starting point for exploring everything else in the old town on foot.</p>\n<h3>The Wider Ciutat Vella Neighbourhood</h3>\n<p>Beyond the immediate cluster of landmarks around Plaza del Ayuntamiento, Ciutat Vella extends north into El Carmen, Valencia's oldest and most atmospheric quarter, where narrow medieval lanes open onto small squares lined with street art, independent galleries, and long-standing bars. Two of the city's surviving medieval gates, Torres de Serranos and Torres de Quart, mark the old boundary of the walled city and are both within a comfortable walk of the hotel, giving a sense of where Valencia's fortifications once stood before most of the walls were demolished in the 19th century to let the city expand.</p>\n<p>A little further south, the Mercado Central occupies a landmark modernist building completed in 1928, its ironwork structure and stained-glass windows ranking it among the larger covered markets in Europe still trading daily. Nearby, the Institut Valencià d'Art Modern (IVAM) houses the city's principal contemporary art collection, giving guests based at Only YOU Hotel Valencia a genuine choice between medieval streets, modernist architecture, and contemporary art without ever needing to leave the old town on foot.</p>\n<h2>Getting There from the Airport</h2>\n<p>The drive in from Valencia Airport follows the main approach roads into the city before joining the avenues that ring Ciutat Vella, a route your driver will have made many times and adjusts according to the time of day. Traffic is heavier during the morning and evening commute and considerably lighter outside those windows, though the final stretch into the pedestrianised core stays relatively unaffected by wider city traffic patterns.</p>\n<p>Rather than negotiating with a taxi rank or working out which bus connects to the old town after landing, the whole journey is arranged before you fly, with the price and pickup details confirmed in advance so there's nothing left to sort out on arrival.</p>\n<h2>Planning Your Transfer</h2>\n<p>Whether you're in Valencia for a short break or the start of a longer trip around the region, booking both directions of your transfer together removes one more thing to think about once you land. See our <a href=\"/valencia/\">Valencia</a> page for more on the city, or <a href=\"/valencia-airport-transfer/\">Valencia airport transfer</a> for general arrival details, and if your stay includes day trips beyond the city, our <a href=\"/hourly-chauffeur/\">hourly chauffeur</a> service covers point-to-point travel on your own schedule.</p>",
